Possible next certificates for the remaining proper groups
==========================================================

This note proposes a continuation of the census. It is neither an executed
checker nor a new case result. The approved subset-family worker concerns
one or two subsets of points, not arbitrary relations on pairs of points.
It must not be relabelled as the latter method.

Let H=<a,b,c> be the actual subgroup of the same original G acting on X.
Any finite object preserved by H but moved by a known element of G proves
H<G. The known moving element must belong to the inner G; an element
known only to belong to Aut(G) is insufficient.

One small instance uses a permutation sigma of X. A supplied certificate
would check every entry of the three equations

  sigma*a=a*sigma, sigma*b=b*sigma, sigma*c=c*sigma,

and exhibit a known delta in G and x in X such that
sigma(delta(x)) != delta(sigma(x)). Then H preserves sigma by conjugation
and G does not. This proves properness even when H is point transitive.
In permutation matrices these are ordinary commutation equations. At
degree3510, sigma needs only7020 bytes as a native unsigned16-bit vector;
there is no need for a dense3510-square matrix or a complete D cache.
A suitable known delta can be the accepted d0 or an authenticated original
A-conjugate of it, using the already accepted inner-group membership leg.

For untrusted discovery, equivariant maps in a transitive action are
determined by the image of one point. Schreier tree lifts give candidates;
point-stabilizer relations eliminate incompatible images. The final
checker must verify the complete permutation and all commutation equations
directly, independently of that search. This route is sufficient, not
complete: a proper subgroup can have the same permutation centralizer as G.

A broader certificate is a nonempty finite relation R in the unordered
point pairs, closed under all three actual generators, together with
p in R and a known delta in G such that delta(p) is outside R. Only closure
and the witnessed failure are required; R need not be proved to be a full
orbit. The possible relation can be much larger than a permutation graph.

Neither numerical method has an approved local implementation at present.
Fresh explicit Source, appropriate synthetic controls and independent
review would be required before any case received credit. No Source
change or group computation was made for this note.
